In article <1992Feb23.215633.20252@ux1.cso.uiuc.edu> act31797@uxa.cso.uiuc.edu (The Procrastinator P1000) writes:
>If you are weak (or hungry, it doesn't matter, but why eat when you don't
>need to?) and start to eat a food ration, but get interrupted by a monster,
>you will still fill up as if you ate the whole ration.  So now you're no
>longer hungry but have a partially-eaten food ration.  Next time you need to
>eat, eat the partial ration, which should function just like a normal ration.
>	Interrupting your eating of a food ration makes it seem as if you
>ate a full ration, then later on ate another full ration.  In other words,
>it appears that each time you just snack on a ration you still get the full
>nutritional benefits.

BUUUUUUUUZZZZZZ!  Totally wrong!

On our system, I've made slight modifications to Nethack so that it shows
how many turns of food you have left on the status line.  Watching that it's
very easy to tell that when you get interrupted while eating a food ration,
you will not have gotten the full nutrition out of it.  Yes, you will not
be hungry any more, but you won't have as many turns to go before you have
to eat again than if you had eaten the whole thing.  The same goes if you
then finish eating that partially eaten food ration.  You will only get
part of the nutrition.  Again - you won't be hungry any more, but you
will have less turns to go before you'll have to eat again than if you'd
eaten a full food ration.

